QUIZ={
"parshat-bamidbar": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Bamidbar opens with a census: every name counted, family by family, banner by banner. Why does a wilderness book begin with a head count?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">To organize the army for the wars ahead</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">To divide the inheritance early</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">Because identity comes from G-d: name by name, house by house</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Shemot is the book of identity. Vayikra is the calling. Bamidbar is where the relationship happens, and it opens by counting names, because before Israel can act, Israel must know who it is. That question, who is Ephraim, is exactly where this teaching goes.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-bereshit": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">This teaching starts in the beginning. But it enters Bereshit through a different chapter entirely. Which one?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Psalm 1, the two ways</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Deuteronomy 30: I have set before you life and death... therefore choose life</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">John 1, in the beginning was the word</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Because the cycle is continuous. Bereshit read through the lens of choice: life and death set before you from the first pages, a famous plural left standing, and room for confusion left on purpose. This teaching asks why He leaves that room.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-chayei-sarah": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">The portion is called Chayei Sarah, the life of Sarah. What is the first thing that happens in it?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Sarah laughs at the promise</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Sarah dies</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">Sarah names her son</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“The life of Sarah... and Sarah died.” The portion named for her life opens with her death, because the Torah measures a life by its fruit and its impact. That is where this teaching goes: what a life leaves behind, and what it consoles.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-eikev": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Eikev means “heel.” The blessing of this portion comes on the heel of something. Of what?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Perfect, flawless obedience</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Hearing, guarding, and doing: walking the relationship</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">The conquest of the land</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Shema, shomer, asah. Three verbs that are a walk, not a score. Torah teaches relationship, not perfection, and the blessing follows on the heel of the walking. Then the teaching asks the question it always returns to: are we that generation?</p></div></div>",
"parshat-emor": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Ezekiel 44:23 gives the priests one teaching job, in a single sentence. What is it?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">To offer the sacrifices at their appointed times</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">To teach My people the difference between holy and common</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">To guard the gates of the house</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The difference between holy and profane, impure and pure. And this teaching asks the uncomfortable question underneath the priests’ job description: where have we made G-d common, and how does the distinction get returned?</p></div></div>",
"parshat-ki-tisa": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">This teaching opens with a sentence built to sting: “Israel loves idolatry. All twelve tribes.” Where does it set the standard for what idolatry even is?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">At the golden calf itself</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">In the prophets’ later indictments</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">At Exodus 20, before the calf ever happened</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The standard comes first: I am Adonai your E-lohim, who brought you out... no other gods, no graven image, no likeness. Only with the standard set can you understand the calf, the tension in the room, and what E-lohim actually means.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-korach": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">This teaching begins not with Korach but with Jeremiah’s diagnosis. Which is the most deceitful thing of all, in Jeremiah 17:9?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">The tongue</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">The heart</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">The eye</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“The heart is the most deceitful of all... who can know it?” Korach’s rebellion did not start in the camp. It started in a blind spot. This teaching is about deceit concealed in truth, testing the teaching without condemning the teacher, and the hidden teshuvah at the end of the story.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-mikeitz": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">“Let my people go, so that they may avodah Me.” What does the word avodah mean?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Worship, and only worship</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">Work, and only work</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">Serve: one word that carries both work and worship</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“Six days you shall avodah and do all of your work.” One word holds both, because dedication is not a feeling. On this rare day, Shabbat, Rosh Chodesh, and Hanukkah at once, this teaching asks what a dedicated life looks like, and whether you will show up.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-nitzavim": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">The covenant of Nitzavim is cut with the people standing there that day. And with whom else, in the text’s own words?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">With the elders and princes only</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Also with those who are not there that day</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">With the generation of Sinai, retroactively</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“And not with you alone... but also with them that are not here with us this day.” Read that again slowly. You were in that sentence. This teaching walks the covenant of Moab, the teshuvah it makes possible, and the day called hayom.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-shelach": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">The tzitzit commandment closes the parashah of the spies. What failure is the fringe the answer to?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">The golden calf</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">Refusing the manna</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">“Following your own heart and your own eyes”</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The spies followed their eyes into fear, and a nation wept all night. The fringe on the garment is the counter-practice, and inside its knots and numbers this teaching finds something more: two houses learning to walk together. One stick in His hand.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-tazria-metzora": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Tazria opens with a woman who has given birth, and the laws of tum’ah that follow. What sin did she commit by giving birth?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">The sin inherited from Eden</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">A ritual failure requiring atonement</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">None. She committed no sin</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>None. “For You formed my inner being... I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” Tazria is about bringing forth life, Metzora about bringing forth evil, and the priestly pattern that handles both. Bad teaching has wounded people here, especially women. This teaching repairs.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-terumah": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">“Speak unto the children of Israel, that they bring Me an offering: from every man that gives it ______.” Complete the verse.</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">According to his means</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Willingly with his heart</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">At the appointed times</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The Mishkan is built only from what the heart gives willingly. And the purpose clause is stranger than we remember: build it so I can dwell in them. In them, not in it. This teaching asks what makes an offering acceptable, from Cain’s field onward.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-vaera": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Pharaoh, the great crocodile of the Nile, said one sentence that made him a god in his own eyes. Which?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\" class=\"pv-correct\">“My river is my own, and I made it for myself”</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">“I am the son of the sun”</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">“Israel shall never leave my hand”</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Four made themselves into gods, and four “they” will come to know. Va’era, “I appeared,” is the Book of Identity meeting the G-d who makes Himself known, in the eyes of the nations. This teaching is about what knowing Him actually means.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-vayeshev": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">In the middle of Joseph’s story, the Torah suddenly interrupts with a whole chapter about someone else. Who?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Benjamin</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Judah</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">Reuben</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“And it came to pass at that time, that Judah went down from his brethren.” The Torah ties Judah to Joseph on purpose, descent beside descent, spark beside spark. This teaching follows that thread all the way to Hanukkah, the holiday of Joseph.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-vayikra": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">The first word of Leviticus, Vayikra, is written with one strange feature in every Torah scroll. What is it?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">An oversized bet</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">A little aleph, written small</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">A doubled yod</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The little aleph turns a word for a chance encounter into a personal, relational calling. And korban was never “sacrifice.” It is drawing near. This teaching carries Isaiah 43’s aching indictment: you stopped calling. And it shows the way to call again.</p></div></div>",
"parshat-yitro": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Jethro, a priest of Midian, hears what G-d did for Israel. What does he do when he hears?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Sends blessings and gifts from afar</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Leaves his home and comes out to the wilderness</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">Warns Moses to return to Midian</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>He heard, and he came. Belief is the starting point, and Moses’ testimony is what we have access to, all five books of it. So this teaching opens with the question Jethro answers: have you heard? And what did you do when you heard?</p></div></div>",
"reading-isaiah-53-honestly": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Before the most argued-over chapter in the Bible begins, how many times does Isaiah name who “My servant” is in the surrounding chapters?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Never. The servant is deliberately unnamed</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\">Once, in passing</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\" class=\"pv-correct\">Four times, by name</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Isaiah 41:8, 44:1-2, 44:21, 49:3. The author introduces his characters before the argued poem, in his own voice. This essay does something rare with Isaiah 53: it reads the chapter honestly, in its own context, with the burden of proof where it belongs. Not an attack. An invitation.</p></div></div>",
"returning-ephraim-to-the-field": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Hosea 5:5 names the thing that makes Israel and Ephraim stumble, and Judah with them. What is it?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Idolatry</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Pride</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">Bloodshed</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>“The pride of Israel testifies to his face.” Pride tore the houses apart, and pride, with its sister jealousy, keeps them apart. This is the hard part of the Elul series: what the prophets say about the relationship, and what Ephraim must actually do to return.</p></div></div>",
"the-king-in-the-field": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">The Alter Rebbe taught that during Elul the King leaves His palace. Where does He go?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">To the Temple courts, to receive offerings</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Into the field, where the laborers are</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">To the city gates, to judge</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>Into the field. No appointment needed, no proper dress required, the King accessible to anyone who will walk out to meet Him. Elul is the season of mercy, and this teaching walks its three great practices, teshuvah, tefillah, tzedakah, while the King is near.</p></div></div>",
"the-temple": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">David tells Nathan he wants to build HaShem a house. What answer comes back that night?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Build it at once, on the mountain I will show you</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">You will not build Mine. I will establish yours, and your seed will build Mine</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">Build a tent, for I have never asked for a house</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>The desire was honored and the timing was refused, and a pattern was set that runs from David through the Straits we mourn in, all the way to Zechariah 8’s picture of what is coming. This teaching, given Between the Straits, traces that pattern.</p></div></div>",
"worthy-is-the-lamb": "<div class=\"pv-quiz\"><p class=\"pv-lbl\">Before You Read</p><p class=\"pv-quiz-lead\">Revelation 5 shows a lamb before the throne, and what follows looks to many readers like divine worship. What question does this essay exist to answer?</p><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1a\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1a\">Who wrote Revelation, and when</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1b\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1b\" class=\"pv-correct\">Does this text require worshipping the lamb alongside G-d?</label><input type=\"radio\" id=\"pvq1c\" name=\"pvq1\"><label for=\"pvq1c\">When is the scroll finally opened?</label><div class=\"pv-quiz-reveal\"><p>For Christians it is a cornerstone. For Messianics, Hebrew Roots, Torah keepers, and Noahides re-examining theology, it is a serious problem. The essay reads the scene against Exodus 20, five passages with one pattern, and the English that is not helping you. Weigh it with the text open.</p></div></div>"
}